{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>A Kiln template is ordinary text, usually HTML, with three kinds of delimiters mixed in. Everything outside them is output as written.</p>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Syntax</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>&#123;{ expression }}</code></td><td>Output the value of an expression</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>&#123;% tag %}</code></td><td>Run a tag, such as <code>if</code> or <code>for</code></td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>&#123;# comment #}</code></td><td>A comment. It produces no output</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	{% apply example(context: { user: { name: "Ada" }, messages: 3 }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{# Greets the signed-in user #}
{% if user %}
	<p>Hello, {{ user.name }}. You have {{ messages }} messages.</p>
{% endif %}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Comments can span several lines, but they don't nest.</p>

	<h2>Whitespace</h2>

	<p>Kiln removes any line that holds only tags or comments, including its line break. Indent your tags to match your HTML, and the output still comes out clean.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { items: ["Tea", "Coffee"] }, format: false) %}
		{% verbatim %}
<ul>
	{% for item in items %}
	<li>{{ item }}</li>
	{% endfor %}
</ul>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Output tags never remove their line, because they print something.</p>

	<p>A byte order mark at the start of a template is ignored, so a file saved with one prints the same as a file without.</p>

	<p>To remove whitespace by hand, add a <code>-</code> inside a delimiter. It removes all whitespace on that side, including line breaks.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { label: "Save" }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
<button>
	{{- label -}}
</button>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Every delimiter supports it: <code>&#123;{-</code>, <code>-}}</code>, <code>&#123;%-</code>, <code>-%}</code>, <code>&#123;#-</code> and <code>-#}</code>.</p>

	<h2>Indentation</h2>

	<p>Kiln removes the extra indentation inside a tag, so you can indent a template for reading without it showing in the HTML.</p>

	{% apply example(context: { items: ["Tea", "Coffee"] }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
<ul>
	{% for item in items %}
		<li>{{ item }}</li>
	{% endfor %}
</ul>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<ul>
		<li>It applies when an opening tag, such as <code>for</code> or <code>if</code>, is on a line of its own.</li>
		<li>Kiln removes the indentation that every line inside shares, beyond the tag's own. If one line is indented less, or uses tabs where the others use spaces, nothing is removed.</li>
		<li>Blank lines don't count.</li>
		<li>Nested tags each remove their own step.</li>
		<li>Content that lines up with its tag, as in the example under Whitespace, is unchanged.</li>
		<li>Only text in the template changes. Values you output keep their own whitespace.</li>
		<li>It happens when the template is compiled, so rendering is no slower.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>Kiln doesn't indent the content that an <code>include</code>, <code>component</code>, <code>slot</code> or <code>block</code> inserts. Each line keeps the indentation it has in its own template, so inserted content starts at the margin.</p>

	{% set card %}
		{% verbatim %}
<article>
	<h2>{{ title }}</h2>
	{% slot %}{% endslot %}
</article>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endset %}
	{% apply example(templates: { "components/card.kiln": card }, format: false) %}
		{% verbatim %}
<main>
	{% component "components/card" with { title: "Hello" } %}
		<p>Welcome back.</p>
	{% endcomponent %}
</main>
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Browsers ignore this whitespace, and indenting every inserted line would slow down every render. To read the HTML of a page, use your browser's developer tools, which show it indented.</p>

	<p>Kiln never changes the indentation of lines inside a <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code>, because there the whitespace is part of the content. A <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> inside an HTML comment, script or style doesn't count.</p>

	<h2>Strings</h2>

	<p>Strings use single or double quotes. Double-quoted strings can include expressions with <code>#{ }</code>:</p>

	{% apply example(context: { user: { name: "ada" }, count: 2 }) %}
		{% verbatim %}
{{ "Hello #{ user.name|title }, you have #{ count } new messages" }}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Both quote styles support the escapes <code>\\</code>, <code>\'</code>, <code>\"</code>, <code>\n</code>, <code>\r</code>, <code>\t</code> and <code>\u{hex}</code>. In a double-quoted string, write <code>\#{</code> for a literal <code>#{</code>. Single-quoted strings never interpolate, so <code>'#{'</code> is already literal there, and <code>\#</code> is an error.</p>

	<h2>Names</h2>

	<p>Variable, function and filter names start with a letter or underscore, followed by letters, digits or underscores. They are case-sensitive. Kiln's own names use camelCase, such as <code>formatDate</code> and <code>loop.revIndex</code>.</p>

	<p>These words are reserved and can't be variable names: <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code>, <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>is</code>, <code>loop</code> and <code>slots</code>.</p>

	<h2>Template names</h2>

	<p>Templates are named by their path, such as <code>pages/home.kiln</code> or <code>components/card</code>.</p>

	<ul>
		<li>The <code>.kiln</code> extension is optional. A name whose last part already holds a dot, such as <code>data/config.json</code>, is used as written, so write <code>partials/nav.main.kiln</code> in full.</li>
		<li>Names starting with <code>./</code> or <code>../</code> are relative to the current template.</li>
		<li>Other names are relative to the loader's root folder.</li>
		<li>A name can't point above the root folder, or name a folder, such as <code>./</code> or <code>partials/</code>.</li>
	</ul>
{% endblock %}
